 -- Marius Schumacher <marius.schumacher@phisys.com>  Mon, 02 Nov 2026 20:10:00 +0100

pvai (0.5.45-1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Performance. The first transcript read skips sidechain lines and
    lines of types nobody reads before parsing, starts with 2 MiB and
    widens to 8 MiB only when that gives fewer than 500 entries, and a
    taken-over session reads its turn state from that same pass (P1).
    Conversation titles are cached per file by mtime and size (P2).
    The project list caches per folder and parses only lines with a cwd
    (P3). An idle session with nothing queued polls its transcript every
    1.5 s and its subagents every 3 s, a hook brings back the fast
    rhythm, and subagents of deleted files are dropped (P4). Broadcasts
    dump the JSON once (P5). The Codex and Kimi message accessors no
    longer copy params and results (P6). WebSocket masks use getrandom
    (P7). The Codex socket handshake times out after 500 ms, retries back
    off to 30 s, and the rollout context is read from the file's end
    (P8). A TLS login parses devices.json once and writes lastSeen at
    most once a minute (P9). The wrapper copies PTY chunks less and stops
    scanning for the trust question once seen (P10).

 -- Marius Schumacher <marius.schumacher@phisys.com>  Mon, 02 Nov 2026 19:45:00 +0100

pvai (0.5.44-1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Logic fixes from an audit. A prompt the wrapper could not type is
    retried after 600 ms and the wrapper sends the screen with its answer
    (A1). A trust dialog answered in the terminal leaves the session idle
    on the next SessionStart (A2). Unix socket peers may send lines up to
    4 MiB (TCP stays 256 KiB) and the hook cuts tool input strings and
    the last answer to 64 KiB (A3). `pvai revoke` fails when the device
    list cannot be written (A4). In a dialog only Return, Esc and digits
    answer; the wrapper's input message carries a key class, an older
    wrapper counts every key (A5). A viewer accepted in a loop round is
    no longer read before it is polled (A6). A lost Codex link answers
    its pending requests with an error (A7). Hooks are deduplicated by
    event, session, tool call and message within 500 ms, not by the whole
    event (A8). An old task notification no longer counts as a running
    turn (A9). Install and logout output without a newline keeps only
    the last 64 KiB (A10). The wrapper removes its .claude file at exit,
    daemon.log and kimi.log rotate above 8 MB, and Codex' stopped threads
    are pruned (A11).

 -- Marius Schumacher <marius.schumacher@phisys.com>  Mon, 02 Nov 2026 14:00:00 +0000

pvai (0.5.43-1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Daemon hardening. A message with a field of the wrong JSON type no
    longer kills the daemon: the peer gets bad-message and is closed (a
    pairing peer counts it as a bad proof); transcript lines of the wrong
    shape are skipped. TLS connections set ACCEPT_MOVING_WRITE_BUFFER and
    ENABLE_PARTIAL_WRITE, so a full socket buffer no longer breaks them.
    runtimeDir refuses a directory that is no private directory of the
    user. A TCP app silent for 60 s, a peer without hello for 10 s and one
    with 4 MB unsent are dropped; accepted TCP sockets keep alive.
    Transcript entries read for history, a dialog or a slash command now
    reach the subscribers too.

